= State of the Art <chap:state-of-the-art>
This chapter presents the current stage of the technologies implemented in this
project: interrupts (#headref(<sec:soa-interrupt>)), timers (#headref(
  <sec:soa-timer>,
), and memory-mapped I/O (#headref(<sec:soa-mmio>), including how they are
implemented in different ISAs and simulators.


== Interrupts <sec:soa-interrupt>
Authors and vendors use different terms to refer to the same concept, a way of
stopping the current execution of the program due to an exceptional situation.
In this thesis, the term _interrupt_ will be used.


=== Interrupt taxonomy <subsec:interrupt-taxonomy>
// bookz
Patterson and Hennery @Patterson2021[chap. 4.10] use the term _exception_ to
refer to #quote[[a]n unscheduled event that disrupts program execution; used to
  detect undefined instructions], defining _interrupt_ as #quote[[a]n exception
  that comes from outside the processor]. Bulić @Bulić2024[chap. 2.3.1], on the
other hand, differentiates between _interrupts_ and _traps_. He defines
interrupts as #quote[signals generated by hardware peripherals or devices to
  signal the processor of an event that requires attention], and distinguishes
between two types: _software_ interrupts, which #quote[function similarly to
  subroutine calls] and #quote[are usually used for requesting operating system
  services], and _external_ interrupts, which are triggered by #quote[[a]n
  external device [...] activating the interrupt request pin on the CPU]. He
further divides the latter into _maskable_, which #quote[can be enabled or
  disabled by setting a bit in the CPU's control register or executing a special
  instruction], and _non-maskable_, which #quote[have a higher priority than
  maskable interrupts]. He also defines _traps_ as #quote[internally generated
  signals that indicate exceptional conditions or errors encountered during the
  execution of program executions] that #quote[require special handling by the
  processor or operating system to resolve and recover from the error].

// universi-titties
Undergraduate courses on computer architecture and organization, such as the
ones from the Northern Illinois University @Winans2021Interrupts and Universidad
Carlos III de Madrid @Carballeira2025Interrupts classify _interrupts_ as either
_synchronous_ or _asynchronous_. _Synchronous_ interrupts are generated when an
instruction fails as a result of an error, or by special system call
instructions, and abort the execution of the current instruction; _asynchronous_
interrupts (also called _interrupt requests_) are generated by either a device,
or a timer, and wait for the instruction to finish. This distinction is mainly
relevant in pipelined architectures, where one instruction is performed during
several clock cycles, and an error during the execution of an instruction
compromises the pipeline and requires immediate attention.

// Z80 & RISC-V
While the Z80 CPU only differentiates between _software maskable interrupts_ and
_nonmaskable interrupts_ @ZilogZ80[p. 17], the RISC-V ISA uses the term
_exception_ as #quote[an unusual condition occurring at run time associated with
  an instruction], while an _interrupt_ is #quote[an external asynchronous event
  that may cause a RISC-V hart to experience an unexpected transfer of control].
It also defines a _trap_ as #quote[the transfer of control to a trap handler
  caused by either an exception or an interrupt], and distinguishes between
_contained_ traps, where #quote[[t]he trap is visible to, and handled by,
  software running inside the execution environment]\; _requested traps_, where
#quote[[t]he trap is a synchronous exception that is an explicit call to the
  execution environment requesting an action on behalf of software inside the
  execution environment], and #quote[execution may or may not resume on the hart
  after the requested action is taken by the execution environment]\; _invisible
traps_, where #quote[[t]he trap is handled transparently by the execution
  environment and execution resumes normally after the trap is handled]\; and a
_fatal trap_, which #quote[represents a fatal failure and causes the execution
  environment to terminate execution] @RISCVUnprivileged[chap. 1.6].


=== Interrupt detection and handling <subsec:interrupt-handling>
// pins
CPUs that support interrupts have an interrupt request (_IRQ_) pin and an
interrupt acknowledge (_INTA_) pin, sometimes mapped to a register. When an
interrupt occurs, the IRQ pin is asserted. As the CPU should finish executing
the current instruction before attending the interrupt, a device may have to
wait for several clock cycles before it is attended, so the INTA pin is used to
signal the device that the CPU has started servicing the interrupt request. The
IRQ pin is typically checked before fetching a new instruction.

// execution context
Before handling the interrupt, it is necessary to save the execution context, so
that the program can be resumed when the interrupt handler has completed. The
execution context varies depending on the architecture, and can include, stack
pointer, status register, and any other registers that are visible to the CPU,
but it always includes at least the program counter. This context can be stored
on special registers, on the stack, or in a combination of both, depending on
the architecture. The cause of the interrupt is also stored in architectures
that support different types of interrupts.

// Execution/Privilege Mode change
Modern architectures, such as Intel's x86 @IntelDevManual3A and RISC-V
@RISCVPrivileged, include _privileged modes_ of execution. These modes enable
multiple operating systems, hypervisors, and security models to be implemented
in the same instruction set architecture, by restricting access to certain
instructions and/or control registers to certain modes. The most basic
implementation includes two modes: a _machine_ mode and a _user_ mode, to
prevent user-level applications to run with restricted access to system
resources, although a third _supervisor_ mode, designed for operating system
kernels and with access to some privileged instructions and registers, is
sometimes implemented. In these architectures, when a mode requires executing an
action of higher privilege, such as accessing hardware devices, it generates an
interrupt (also called _trap_ in this specific case) that changes the execution
mode. Interrupts generated by other methods, such as errors during the
execution, also trigger this change.

// handler
Different types of interrupts require different procedures to handle them,
therefore there must be a mechanism to execute the correct one for each type.
There are two approaches to this problem: _polled interrupts_ and _vectored
interrupts_. In the former, the CPU branches to a specific handler address that
typically evaluates the type of interrupt and branches to the handler for the
specific type. The latter---as shown in #ref(
  <fig:vectored-interrupts>,
)---implements an _interrupt vector table_, a table containing _interrupt
vectors_, addresses of interrupt handlers, typically managed by the operating
system. The CPU jumps to each entry specified in the _interrupt register_. This
register is typically divided into the base address of the interrupt vector
table and the interrupt type, or interrupt exception code, allowing users to
specify the address of the table. Once the handler has finished, usually
signaled by some kind of special "return from interrupt" instruction, the CPU
must restore the previous execution context and privilege mode. Most
architectures include a special instruction to return from an interrupt with
this functionality.

